# chaos.pack: a deterministic store-only ZIP of the whole client tree (init.lua, data/, modules/, mods/, layouts/)
# encrypted as one blob with AES-256-GCM (OpenSSL, which the client links already). The engine decrypts it in place into one buffer (no copies)
# and mounts it through the existing PhysFS memory-archive path, so every module keeps reading files through
# g_resources unchanged and the shipped folder is just the exe and this one file.
#   * src/framework/util/chaospack.h (new, header only; vectors in client/tools/chaospack_test.cpp): AES-256-GCM
#     encrypt/decrypt and HKDF-SHA256 key derivation, byte-identical to the packing tool client/tools/pack_client.py.
#     The pack header (magic, version, flags, 12-byte nonce, plaintext length) is the GCM AAD, so any tamper fails.
#   * src/framework/core/resourcemanager.{h,cpp}: mountEncryptedPack(key) finds chaos.pack beside the exe, decrypts it
#     with the key and mounts it from memory (mountMemoryData); setup() and getCompactName() use the mounted pack.
#     A wrong key or a corrupt pack is fatal; no pack present (a dev tree of loose files) returns false and loads as
#     before.
#   * src/main.cpp: before init.lua is read, derive the pack key from the client guard key (chaosguard::builtInKey(),
#     base patch 0035 - no new secret) and mount the pack, then wipe the key. The app name is "CindersOfChaos", so the
#     per-user write dir (config.otml, logs, the updater tree) is named after it, never next to the exe.
#   * init.lua: APP_NAME "CindersOfChaos" (the compact name, config dir and <name>.log file).
# This deters copying; it does not stop a determined reverse engineer, since the key is in the binary (obfuscated the
# way chaosguard_key.h is). Apply after 0001-0101: tools/assemble_client.sh applies every patch in order.
